    "tapAgain": "Tap any card on the grid to hear the pair again.",
    "title": "Opposites",
    "vs": "vs",
    "detail": {
      "bigSmall": {
        "about": "{first} and {second} are opposites about size. A {first} thing takes up lots of room — like an elephant or a big pillow. A {second} thing fits easily in your hand — like a mouse or a marble. When you compare two toys, one can be bigger and the other smaller.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Your shoe looks {first} next to a baby’s tiny sock! You are bigger than when you were a baby, but still smaller than most grown-ups.",
        "try": "With a grown-up, find one {first} object and one {second} object in your room. Hold them side by side, point, and say “{first}!” then “{second}!”"
      },
      "hotCold": {
        "about": "{first} means very warm — like soup on the stove or sunshine on your back. {second} means very cool — like ice cubes or winter air on your cheeks. They are opposites for temperature, so your body feels them differently.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Warm baths feel {first} and a cold popsicle feels {second} on your tongue! Your hands can tell {first} and {second} before you even look.",
        "try": "Ask a grown-up for something safe and warm (not too hot) and something cool. Touch gently and whisper “{first}” … “{second}.”"
      },
      "dayNight": {
        "about": "{first} is when the sun is up, the sky is bright, and most people go to school or play outside. {second} is when the sun rests, stars can shine, and your body often wants sleep. They are opposites for time in a full day.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Some animals sleep in the {second} and wake up in the {first} — like owls! You probably play more in the {first} and rest in the {second}.",
        "try": "Draw a sun for {first} and a moon for {second}. Say both words slowly and tell which one it is right now outside."
      },
      "happySad": {
        "about": "{first} feels bright inside — smiles, giggles, and wanting to play. {second} can feel heavy — quiet tears, wanting a hug, or resting. Both feelings are normal; they are opposites in your heart, not “good” or “bad” people.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Everyone feels {first} and {second} sometimes, even grown-ups! Talking to someone you trust helps {second} feelings soften.",
        "try": "Make a {first} face in a mirror, then a gentle {second} face. Say “{first}” and “{second}” and tell a grown-up one time you felt each."
      },
      "fastSlow": {
        "about": "{first} means moving quickly — like a rabbit running or you sprinting to the door. {second} means moving gently — like a turtle walking or you tiptoeing. They are opposites for speed.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? A cheetah is very {first}, but a snail is very {second}! You can be {first} in a race and {second} when you need to be careful on stairs.",
        "try": "Jog in place for five steps and shout “{first}!” Then walk slowly and whisper “{second}!” Which feels safer on slippery floors?"
      },
      "tallShort": {
        "about": "{first} means reaching high — like a giraffe’s neck or a grown-up standing next to you. {second} means closer to the ground — like a hamster or you sitting on the floor. They compare height, not how special someone is.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Plants can be {first} like trees or {second} like grass! You will grow taller each year, but you are perfect at your height today.",
        "try": "Stand next to a grown-up: point up and say “{first}!” Sit on your knees and say “{second}!” Draw two stick people — one {first}, one {second}."
      },
      "upDown": {
        "about": "{first} means toward the sky — balloons rise, you jump, and birds fly {first}. {second} means toward the floor — leaves fall, raindrops drip, and you sit {second}. They are opposite directions.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Elevators go up and down to different floors! On the playground, the slide goes down while you climb up the ladder.",
        "try": "Reach your arms up and say “{first}!” Touch your toes toward the floor and say “{second}!” Do it three times slowly."
      },
      "fullEmpty": {
        "about": "{first} means no room left — like a cup filled with milk or a bag packed with toys. {second} means nothing inside — like a cup before pouring or a box with no toys. They are opposites for how much is inside.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Your tummy feels {first} after a good meal and {second} before breakfast! A {second} backpack is light to carry; a {first} one can feel heavy.",
        "try": "With a grown-up, show a full cup and an empty cup. Say “{first}” and “{second}.” Pack a small box, then empty it and compare."
      },
      "cleanDirty": {
        "about": "{first} means washed and fresh — clean hands before eating, clean clothes after a bath. {second} means mud, crumbs, or marks — like shoes after puddles. They are opposites for how neat something looks.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Soap and water turn {second} hands into {first} hands! Pets get {second} paws outside, then love a gentle wipe.",
        "try": "Wash your hands with a grown-up until they feel {first}. Say “{first}!” Remember muddy shoes are {second} — say “{second}!” and help wipe them."
      },
      "loudQuiet": {
        "about": "{first} means a big sound — drums, sirens, or shouting in a gym. {second} means a soft sound — whispering, sleeping babies, or library voices. Your ears notice the difference right away.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Thunder can be {first} while falling snow is very {second}! Libraries need {second} voices so everyone can read.",
        "try": "Clap once loudly and say “{first}!” Then clap softly and whisper “{second}!” Practice {second} voice before bedtime stories."
      },
      "openClosed": {
        "about": "{first} means you can go in or see inside — an open door, an open book, an open jar. {second} means shut tight — a closed door, a closed lid, a closed mouth when eating politely. They are opposites for “can I get in?”",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Flowers can be open in sunshine and closed at night! Your eyes are open when awake and often closed when you sleep.",
        "try": "Open a book and say “{first}!” Close it gently and say “{second}!” Try with a door only if a grown-up says it is safe."
      },
      "lightDark": {
        "about": "{first} means bright — lamps, sunshine, and flashlights help you see colors clearly. {second} means little light — shadows, night sky, or a room before you turn on a lamp. They are opposites for how much you can see.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? The moon does not make its own light — it reflects sunshine! Fireflies glow in the dark like tiny living stars.",
        "try": "Turn on a small lamp: say “{first}!” Turn it off (with a grown-up) and say “{second}!” Notice how shapes are easier with light."
      },
      "goodBad": {
        "about": "We use {first} for kind, helpful, or tasty things — a good friend, good manners, good food. We use {second} for things that hurt, break rules, or taste yucky — not for people, but for choices or weather. They are opposites for “how nice is this?”",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Even when you make a bad choice by mistake, you can try again and do something good! Weather can be bad for picnics but good for cozy reading.",
        "try": "Name one {first} thing you did today and say “{first}!” If you spilled something, that was a small {second} moment — say “{second}” and tell how you fixed it."
      },
      "nearFar": {
        "about": "{first} means close — your nose to a book, a friend on the same bench. {second} means a long way — a mountain on the horizon, an airplane tiny in the sky. They are opposites for distance.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Your voice sounds near when whispering to a friend and far when shouting across a big field! Stars look far but the moon feels closer.",
        "try": "Touch something {first} and say “{first}!” Point to something across the room or window and say “{second}!” Take three steps and notice distance change."
      },
      "youngOld": {
        "about": "{first} means little age — babies, puppies, and you when you were smaller. {second} means many years — grandparents, old trees, antique clocks. They describe time alive, not “better” or “worse.”",
        "funFact": "Did you know? A young chick becomes a grown chicken, and a young tree can live until it is very old! You are young now and learn new things every year.",
        "try": "Look at a baby photo: say “I was {first}!” Hug a grandparent or look at their picture and say “{second}!” with respect."
      },
      "softHard": {
        "about": "{first} feels gentle when you squeeze — stuffed animals, pillows, cotton balls. {second} feels firm — rocks, wooden blocks, a frozen ice cube. Your fingers learn opposites by touch.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Bread is soft inside but the crust can feel hard! Your bed is soft; the floor is hard if you hop without shoes.",
        "try": "Touch something {first} and say “{first}!” Touch something hard safely (like a book cover) and say “{second}!” Which would you nap on?"
      },
      "sweetSour": {
        "about": "{first} tastes sugary and yummy — ripe mango, candy, honey on toast. {second} tastes sharp on your tongue — lemon, unripe gooseberry, some pickles. They are taste opposites.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Many fruits start a little sour and become sweet when ripe! Your tongue has tiny helpers that tell sweet from sour.",
        "try": "With a grown-up, taste something {first} and say “{first}!” Then a tiny safe {second} bite (like lemon) and make a funny face — “{second}!”"
      },
      "wetDry": {
        "about": "{first} means water is on it — rain on hair, wet paint, splashed puddles. {second} means no water — towels after the sun, dry sand, warm clothes from the line. They are opposites for water.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Dogs shake to go from wet to dry fast! Paint must stay wet while you brush, then it turns dry and hard.",
        "try": "Wet a sponge (with help), say “{first}!” Squeeze it in a towel until dry and say “{second}!” Never touch plugs when wet."
      },
      "thickThin": {
        "about": "{first} means wide from side to side — a thick book, a thick crayon, a thick sandwich. {second} means slim — one sheet of paper, a thin string, a thin pancake. They compare how “fat” the edge is.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Tree trunks are thick but leaves are thin! A thick blanket is cozy; thin paper is good for folding planes.",
        "try": "Stack three books: say “{first}!” Hold one sheet: say “{second}!” Compare with your fingers like a sandwich."
      },
      "heavyLight": {
        "about": "{first} is hard to lift — a full backpack, a big rock, a sleeping grown-up who needs two people to carry jokingly. {second} is easy to lift — a balloon, a feather, an empty cup. They are opposites for weight.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? A big balloon looks big but feels light! Lead feels heavy even when small — always lift with a grown-up.",
        "try": "Try lifting a light toy with one hand — say “{second}!” Try a heavy thing only with a grown-up — say “{first}!” and bend your knees."
      },
      "startEnd": {
        "about": "{first} is where something begins — the first page, the starting line, “ready, set…” {second} is where it finishes — the last page, the finish line, bedtime after the story. They are opposites for beginning and finishing.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Every song has a start and an end! Movies start with lights down and end with credits rolling.",
        "try": "At “{first}” of a race, crouch. At “end” cross your arms like finishing. Read the {first} and last line of a short book."
      },
      "frontBack": {
        "about": "{first} is what you face — the front of your shirt, the front door you knock on. {second} is behind — the back of your head, the back seat of a car. They are opposite sides.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Coins have a front picture and a back picture! Backpacks go on your back while your smile is on the front.",
        "try": "Point to your front (chest) and say “{first}!” Point behind you and say “{second}!” Walk around a chair and name each side."
      },
      "insideOutside": {
        "about": "{first} means within something — toys in a box, juice inside a fruit, you inside a house. {second} means beyond the walls — playground, garden, birds in the sky. They are place opposites.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Seeds grow inside fruit, then plants live outside in soil! You wear clothes inside and jackets outside when it is cold.",
        "try": "Put a toy inside a box — say “{first}!” Take it out — say “{second}!” Stand in a doorway: one foot inside, one outside."
      },
      "winLose": {
        "about": "{first} means you reached the goal first or got the most points — hooray! {second} means the other team or friend scored more this time. Both happen in games; being kind matters more than always {first}.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Even champions lose sometimes — then they practice! You can win a board game and still hug the friend who lost.",
        "try": "Play a quick game with a grown-up. If you win, say “good game!” If you lose, smile and say “{second} is okay — let’s try again!”"
      },
      "giveTake": {
        "about": "{first} means you let someone have something — sharing a crayon, giving a gift. {second} means you receive — taking a turn, accepting help politely. They are opposites for moving things between people.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Birthday parties are full of give and thank-you for take! Plants give oxygen and take sunlight.",
        "try": "Give a toy to a friend or grown-up and say “{first}!” Take it back gently with “thank you” — say “{second}!” Practice polite words."
      },
      "pushPull": {
        "about": "{first} means moving something away from you — pushing a swing, a door, a shopping cart. {second} means bringing it closer — pulling a wagon, a rope, a drawer handle. Forces go opposite ways.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? A door can say “push” or “pull” — read the sign before you tug! Wagons need pull; strollers are often pushed.",
        "try": "Push a chair safely with a grown-up — “{first}!” Pull it back — “{second}!” Feel how your muscles work differently."
      },
      "highLow": {
        "about": "{first} is up high — a kite in the sky, a shelf you cannot reach, a high jump. {second} is down low — ants on the ground, shoes on the floor, a low whisper. They are height opposites (like up and down, but we say high/low too).",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Birds fly high and worms crawl low! Your voice can sing a high note or a low note.",
        "try": "Jump and reach high — “{first}!” Crouch low like a crab — “{second}!” Hum a high sound, then a low sound."
      },
      "moreLess": {
        "about": "{first} means a bigger amount — more crackers on the plate, more blocks in the tower. {second} means a smaller amount — less juice, fewer turns left. They help you compare numbers and shares.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? If you eat more crackers, less stay on the plate! Sharing can turn more for you into more smiles for everyone.",
        "try": "Put five buttons in one pile (more) and two in another (less). Say “{first}” and “{second}!” Share one button and watch piles change."
      },
      "sameDifferent": {
        "about": "{first} means matching — twin socks, same color, same shape. {second} means not alike — a cat and a bus, red and blue. Opposites help you sort and notice patterns.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Twins can look the same but still like different foods! Snowflakes are all different shapes up close.",
        "try": "Find two matching socks — “{first}!” Find two different toys — “{second}!” Draw two same circles, then one square."
      },
      "rightWrong": {
        "about": "{first} means correct — the right answer on homework, the right shoe on the right foot. {second} means a mistake — wrong puzzle piece, wrong turn on a path. Mistakes help you learn; they do not mean you are bad.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Erasers fix wrong pencil marks! A right turn on a map is a direction — different from wrong answers on homework.",
        "try": "Solve an easy puzzle piece: “{first}!” Flip one on purpose to see wrong — “{second}!” — then fix it and cheer."
      },
      "leftRight": {
        "about": "{first} and {second} are sides of your body — left hand waves, right hand waves. They are direction opposites, like on roads and in games.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Many people write with the right hand, but some use the left — both are fine! Traffic drives on different sides in different countries.",
        "try": "Raise your left hand — say “{first}!” Raise your right — say “{second}!” Stamp left foot, then right foot slowly."
      },
      "prettyPlain": {
        "about": "{first} means extra beautiful — sparkly dress, colorful flowers, decorated cake. {second} means simple — plain white cup, plain brown box, no extra patterns. Both can be useful and okay.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Butterflies are pretty and plain rocks can still be special for collecting! Plain notebooks are great for drawing your own art.",
        "try": "Point to something pretty — say “{first}!” Point to something plain — say “{second}!” Draw a plain circle, then decorate one half."
      },
      "easyHard": {
        "about": "{first} means you can do it without much struggle — easy puzzle, easy hop. {second} means you need practice, help, or time — hard spelling word, hard climb. Trying hard things makes your brain stronger.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Riding a bike felt hard at first, then became easier! Grown-ups still learn hard new skills too.",
        "try": "Do an easy clap pattern — “{first}!” Try a harder one slowly with a grown-up — “{second}!” Say “I can practice.”"
      },
      "kindMean": {
        "about": "{first} is gentle and caring — sharing, helping, using nice words. {second} hurts feelings — yelling names, grabbing, leaving someone out. We practice {first} to keep friends safe and happy.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? One kind sentence can fix a sad friend! Even good kids must learn not to be mean when angry.",
        "try": "Act out a kind hello — “{first}!” Talk about a mean cartoon villain — “{second} is not for real friends.” Plan one kind act today."
      },
      "braveScared": {
        "about": "{first} means trying even when you feel a little afraid — new school, dark room with a night-light, speaking in class. {second} means your body wants to hide — loud thunder, big dog, first swim lesson. You can be brave and still feel scared sometimes.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Firefighters feel scared too, but train to help! Holding a grown-up’s hand can turn scared into brave steps.",
        "try": "Tell one thing that feels scary — “{second}.” Take one small brave step (like looking at a picture of the thing) and say “{first}!”"
      },
      "awakeAsleep": {
        "about": "{first} means eyes open, mind ready — playing, eating, learning. {second} means resting — closed eyes, dreams, quiet breathing. Your body needs both every day.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Cats nap many times but still wake up awake quickly! Yawning can mean you are moving from awake toward asleep.",
        "try": "Stretch wide like morning — “{first}!” Put hands on cheeks and fake snore gently — “{second}!” Talk about your bedtime routine."
      },
      "sharpRound": {
        "about": "{first} means pointy corners or edges — triangle tip, scissors (grown-ups only), pine needle. {second} means smooth curves — ball, wheel, coin. Touch carefully; {first} things can prick.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Pizza slices look sharp at the tip but the crust is more round! Stars in drawings are sharp; wheels are round.",
        "try": "Find a round plate — “{second}!” Find a sharp pencil point only by looking — “{first}!” — do not poke skin."
      },
      "smoothRough": {
        "about": "{first} feels even and slippery — glass, polished stone, silk scarf. {second} feels bumpy — tree bark, sandpaper, brick wall. Skin learns opposites fast.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Dolphins feel smooth and cat tongues feel a little rough! Ice cream is smooth; a coconut shell outside is rough.",
        "try": "Rub a smooth spoon on your hand — “{first}!” Touch safe rough bark — “{second}!” with a grown-up."
      },
      "wideNarrow": {
        "about": "{first} means stretching far sideways — wide road, wide river, arms spread wide. {second} means skinny — narrow path, narrow bridge, pencil line. They are width opposites.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? You can sing with a wide open mouth; a narrow straw sips juice! Mice in stories use narrow holes; buses need wide roads.",
        "try": "Make your arms wide — “{first}!” Bring elbows in — “{second}!” Walk on a wide line vs a thin tape line on the floor."
      },
      "earlyLate": {
        "about": "{first} means before the usual time — early bird, early bus, sunrise. {second} means after the time — late bedtime, late arrival, sunset after expected hour. Clocks help us tell them apart.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Being early to school gives calm time; being late makes you rush! Some flowers open early in the morning.",
        "try": "Pretend alarm rings early — stretch and say “{first}!” Pretend you missed the bell — tiptoe fast and say “{second}!” — then talk about planning ahead."
      },
      "comeGo": {
        "about": "{first} means moving toward — come here, come inside, come to the table. {second} means moving away — go play outside, go to school, go home. They are direction opposites for people.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Dogs come when called and go when the door opens! “Come” usually means closer to the speaker; “go” means farther away.",
        "try": "Ask a grown-up to say “{first} here!” — walk toward them. Then “{second} back!” — walk away safely. Say both words clearly."
      },
      "firstLast": {
        "about": "{first} is before everyone else in line or order — first birthday candle blown, first place in a friendly race. last is at the end — last bite of cake, last person in queue. Neither means “best person” — just order.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? The ABC song has a first letter A and a last letter Z! Sometimes the last friend in line gets a funny hat at parties.",
        "try": "Line up toys: point to {first} — “{first}!” Point to last — “{second}!” Switch places and see order change."
      },
      "strongWeak": {
        "about": "{first} means powerful muscles or tough material — strong hero, strong bridge, strong wind. {second} means gentle or easy to bend — weak tea (for grown-ups), weak squeak, baby plant stem. Bodies grow stronger with food, sleep, and play.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Ants are tiny but strong for their size! A weak paper bridge holds only light toys until you reinforce it.",
        "try": "Flex muscles safely — “{first}!” Floppy rag doll arms — “{second}!” Eat healthy food to stay strong."
      },
      "sunnyRainy": {
        "about": "{first} means bright sun, shadows on the ground, sunglasses sometimes. {second} means clouds dropping rain, puddles, umbrellas. Sky opposites change what you wear outside.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Rain helps plants grow after sunny days! You can have a rainy morning and a sunny afternoon — weather changes.",
        "try": "Draw a sunny sky and a rainy sky. Say “{first}” and “{second}!” Look out the window — which is today?"
      },
      "summerWinter": {
        "about": "{first} is the hot season — swimming, ice cream, light clothes in many places. {second} is the cold season — sweaters, hot soup, sometimes snow. They are opposite seasons in a year.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? When it is summer in one place, another country might have winter! Animals grow thick fur before winter.",
        "try": "Pretend to fan yourself — “{first}!” Wrap arms like a cozy coat — “{second}!” Name one fun thing for each season."
      },
      "boyGirl": {
        "about": "{first} and {second} are words some people use for children. All kids can like any color, game, or toy. Opposites here are about words, not about who is smarter or kinder.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Boys and girls both can be doctors, dancers, and astronauts! Kindness matters more than the label.",
        "try": "Draw two smiling kids — say “{first}” and “{second}!” List games you both like — notice many are the same."
      },
      "messyNeat": {
        "about": "{first} means toys everywhere, spilled crumbs, crayons mixed up. {second} means tidy shelves, beds made, blocks sorted. Rooms go from {first} to {second} when you help clean.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Art projects look messy while creating and neat when tools are put away! Even neat desks get messy during fun.",
        "try": "Make a tiny messy pile of socks, then sort into a neat pile — say both words! Sing a cleanup song with a grown-up."
      },
      "laughCry": {
        "about": "{first} is happy sound — giggles, belly laughs, jokes. {second} is sad or hurt sound — tears, sniffles, needing comfort. Both are normal ways bodies show feelings.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Laughing can be contagious — one giggle starts more! Crying can help eyes after dust; grown-ups hug you when you cry.",
        "try": "Do a gentle laugh — “{first}!” Hug a pillow for a quiet sad face — “{second}!” Talk about a funny vs sad cartoon."
      },
      "stopGo": {
        "about": "{first} means freeze — red light, stop sign, “stop!” before crossing street. {second} means move — green light, “go!” on a race, walking when safe. They keep us safe on roads.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Games like “red light, green light” use stop and go! Buses stop at stations then go again.",
        "try": "Play red-light-green-light: stop like a statue — “{first}!” Go when safe — “{second}!” Only where you have space."
      },
      "sittingStanding": {
        "about": "{first} means bottom on chair, floor, or bench — story time, eating. {second} means feet hold you up — line at school, waving hello. Muscles work differently for each.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Birds stand on one leg sometimes and sit on nests! You sit in class and stand for songs or assembly in many schools.",
        "try": "Sit down slowly — “{first}!” Stand up tall — “{second}!” Repeat five times like gentle exercise."
      }
    }
  },
  "shapes": {
    "colorMessage": "This color is called {name}.",
    "header": "Learn Shapes & Colors",
    "shapeMessage": "This shape is called {name}.",
    "tabColors": "Colors",
    "tabShapes": "Shapes",
    "tapColor": "Tap a color to say its name.",
    "tapShape": "Tap a shape to say its name.",
    "names": {
      "circle": "Circle",
      "square": "Square",
      "triangle": "Triangle",
      "rectangle": "Rectangle",
      "diamond": "Diamond",
      "cube": "Cube",
      "star": "Star",
      "heart": "Heart",
      "oval": "Oval",
      "box": "Box",
      "line": "Line",
      "pentagon": "Pentagon",
      "hexagon": "Hexagon",
      "octagon": "Octagon",
      "cloudShape": "Cloud shape"
    },
    "colorNames": {
      "red": "Red",
      "yellow": "Yellow",
      "black": "Black",
      "purple": "Purple",
      "white": "White",
      "darkGreen": "Dark green",
      "brown": "Brown",
      "darkOrange": "Dark orange",
      "gold": "Gold",
      "grey": "Grey",
      "blue": "Blue",
      "pink": "Pink",
      "silver": "Silver",
      "orange": "Orange",
      "darkBlue": "Dark blue",
      "lightGreen": "Light green",
      "skyBlue": "Sky blue",
      "green": "Green"
    },
    "detail": {
      "circle": {
        "about": "A circle is a round shape with no corners and no straight sides. Wheels, coins, plates, and the sun in drawings often look like circles.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Every point on a circle is the same distance from the center. That is why wheels are circular - they roll smoothly.",
        "try": "Draw a big circle in the air with your finger and say \"{name}!\" Then find one round thing nearby."
      },
      "square": {
        "about": "A square has four equal sides and four corners. Floor tiles, window blocks, and some toy boxes are square.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? If all four sides are equal and all corners are right angles, it is a square. Squares stack neatly.",
        "try": "Use your finger to draw 4 sides: up, across, down, across. Say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "triangle": {
        "about": "A triangle has three sides and three corners. Pizza slices, road signs, and party flags can look like triangles.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? The strongest bridge frames often use triangles because they hold shape well.",
        "try": "Make a triangle with both hands and say \"{name}!\" Then count its 3 corners."
      },
      "rectangle": {
        "about": "A rectangle has four corners and opposite sides that match in length. Books, doors, notebooks, and phone screens are often rectangles.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? A rectangle is like a stretched square. Many rooms and tables are rectangle-shaped.",
        "try": "Trace a rectangle in front of you and say \"{name}!\" Find one in your room."
      },
      "diamond": {
        "about": "A diamond shape looks like a tilted square with four equal sides. Kite patterns and playing card diamonds use this shape.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? The diamond shape is also called a rhombus in math class.",
        "try": "Tilt your square hand-shape and say \"{name}!\" Draw one like a kite."
      },
      "cube": {
        "about": "A cube is a 3D shape with six equal square faces. Dice, toy blocks, and ice cubes are examples of cubes.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? A cube has 8 corners and 12 edges. It is a solid shape, not a flat one.",
        "try": "Hold an imaginary block in your hands and say \"{name}!\" Then find a box-like object."
      },
      "star": {
        "about": "A star shape has pointed tips and lines that meet at corners. Kids draw stars in sky pictures and celebrations.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? The stars in space are actually giant balls of hot gas, but we draw them with points.",
        "try": "Draw a 5-point star in the air and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "heart": {
        "about": "A heart shape has two rounded tops and a point at the bottom. It is used in art to show love and care.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Real hearts in our body do not look exactly like the heart symbol, but the symbol is easy to recognize.",
        "try": "Make a heart with your fingers and say \"{name}!\" Think of one person you love."
      },
      "oval": {
        "about": "An oval is a stretched circle, like an egg shape. Eggs, some mirrors, and race tracks can look oval.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? If you press a circle from two sides, it becomes more oval.",
        "try": "Draw an egg-like shape in the air and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "box": {
        "about": "A box shape in this lesson means a square or rectangular frame. Gift boxes, lunch boxes, and storage boxes use this form.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Boxes are designed to hold things safely and stack one above another.",
        "try": "Pretend to open a box lid and say \"{name}!\" Name one thing you keep in a box."
      },
      "line": {
        "about": "A line is a straight path from one point to another. Roads, rulers, and notebook margins use lines.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Lines can be horizontal, vertical, or slanting. Shapes are made by joining lines.",
        "try": "Draw one long straight line with your finger and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "pentagon": {
        "about": "A pentagon has five sides and five corners. Some badges and building designs use pentagon shapes.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? If you count carefully, every pentagon always has 5 edges.",
        "try": "Count 1 to 5 while tracing a pentagon in the air and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "hexagon": {
        "about": "A hexagon has six sides and six corners. Honeycomb cells made by bees are famous hexagon shapes.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Hexagons fit together without gaps, so bees use them to store honey efficiently.",
        "try": "Pretend to draw a honeycomb cell and say \"{name}!\" Count 6 sides."
      },
      "octagon": {
        "about": "An octagon has eight sides and eight corners. Stop signs in many countries are octagons.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? The word octagon starts with \"octa,\" which means eight.",
        "try": "Hold up 8 fingers while saying \"{name}!\" Then imagine a stop sign."
      },
      "cloudShape": {
        "about": "A cloud shape is soft and puffy with curved bumps. Kids often draw clouds as rounded fluffy forms in the sky.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Real clouds are made of tiny water droplets, but in drawing we use fun cloud outlines.",
        "try": "Make a fluffy cloud shape in the air and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "black": {
        "about": "Black is a very dark color, like night sky without city lights. Blackboards, tires, and some shoes are black.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Black absorbs more heat from sunlight than many lighter colors.",
        "try": "Point to one black object nearby and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "white": {
        "about": "White is a very light and clean-looking color. Clouds, milk, chalk, and many flowers can be white.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? White reflects a lot of light, so white clothes can feel cooler in hot sun.",
        "try": "Find one white thing in the room and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "grey": {
        "about": "Grey is between black and white. Clouds, stones, and metal objects are often grey.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Mixing black and white paint gives different shades of grey.",
        "try": "Show a soft grey color with your pencil or imagine rainy clouds and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "red": {
        "about": "Red is a bright, warm color seen in apples, roses, and stop signs. It stands out quickly.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Red often signals attention, like traffic stop lights and warning signs.",
        "try": "Name one red fruit and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "orange": {
        "about": "Orange is a cheerful color between red and yellow. Oranges, marigold flowers, and evening skies can look orange.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Orange is often used for safety cones because it is easy to spot.",
        "try": "Pretend to peel an orange fruit and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "yellow": {
        "about": "Yellow is a bright sunny color. Bananas, lemons, and sunshine drawings are often yellow.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Yellow is one of the easiest colors to notice from far away.",
        "try": "Draw a tiny sun in the air and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "green": {
        "about": "Green is the color of many leaves, grass, and plants. It reminds us of nature and fresh growth.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Plants look green because of chlorophyll, which helps them make food from sunlight.",
        "try": "Look for a plant or leaf and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "blue": {
        "about": "Blue is a calm color often seen in sky and water. Many uniforms, bags, and toys are blue.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Oceans can look blue because water reflects and scatters sunlight.",
        "try": "Point up to the sky (or imagine it) and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "skyBlue": {
        "about": "Sky blue is a light shade of blue, like a clear daytime sky. It feels soft and airy.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Artists use sky blue to paint peaceful weather and open spaces.",
        "try": "Spread your arms wide like open sky and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "lightGreen": {
        "about": "Light green is a fresh shade of green, like new leaves or spring grass. It looks bright and lively.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? New plant leaves are often lighter green than old leaves.",
        "try": "Imagine tiny new leaves growing and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "darkOrange": {
        "about": "Dark orange is a deeper shade of orange, like autumn leaves or evening marigolds.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Dark orange is used in signs and sports gear because it remains bold and visible.",
        "try": "Pretend to color a pumpkin and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "darkBlue": {
        "about": "Dark blue is a deeper, richer blue like late evening sky or school uniforms. It feels strong and calm.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Navy blue is a popular dark blue used in uniforms and formal clothes.",
        "try": "Close your eyes and imagine night sky, then say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "darkGreen": {
        "about": "Dark green is a deep green shade found in thick forests and big tree leaves.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Dark green shades are often used to show trees and nature in maps and drawings.",
        "try": "Pretend to be a tall tree and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "purple": {
        "about": "Purple is a color made by mixing red and blue. Grapes, lavender flowers, and some dresses can be purple.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? In old times, purple dye was rare and expensive, so it was linked with royalty.",
        "try": "Name one purple fruit or flower and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "pink": {
        "about": "Pink is a soft, playful color often seen in flowers, candies, and toys.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Pink is usually made by adding white to red, creating a lighter shade.",
        "try": "Draw a tiny pink heart in the air and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "brown": {
        "about": "Brown is an earthy color seen in tree trunks, soil, chocolate, and wooden furniture.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Many animals use brown fur or feathers to blend into nature.",
        "try": "Touch a wooden object or imagine soil and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "gold": {
        "about": "Gold is a shiny yellow-metal color that looks rich and bright. Trophies, stars, and festive decorations may look gold.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Real gold is a metal that does not rust easily.",
        "try": "Pretend to wear a gold crown and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "silver": {
        "about": "Silver is a cool shiny grey-metal color. Spoons, foil, and some stars in drawings look silver.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Silver is a real metal used in jewelry and special electronics.",
        "try": "Pretend to polish a silver spoon and say \"{name}!\""
      }
    }
  },
