    "subtitleSeason": "Spring, summer, autumn, winter, monsoon — tap to hear.",
    "subtitleWeather": "Everyday sky words — tap a card to hear the name.",
    "tabSeason": "Seasons",
    "tabWeather": "Weather",
    "tapAgain": "Close this box and tap the same card to hear the word again.",
    "title": "Weather & seasons",
    "detail": {
      "sunny": {
        "about": "Sunny weather means the sun is shining and the sky looks bright. You may feel warm on your skin, see sharp shadows, and want to play outside. Sunny days help plants grow because they use sunlight to make food.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? The sun is a star very far away — its light still reaches Earth! On very sunny days, drinking water, wearing a hat, and sitting in shade help your body stay cool.",
        "try": "Hold your hand above your eyes like a little visor. Say \"{name}!\" and name one sunny thing you like — bright sky, warm light, or long shadows."
      },
      "rainy": {
        "about": "Rainy weather means water falls from clouds as raindrops. The sky may look gray, the ground gets wet, and you might hear pitter-patter on roofs and windows. Rain fills rivers and helps plants and animals get the water they need.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Rain starts when tiny drops inside clouds join together until they are heavy enough to fall! Some rainy days are soft and gentle; others bring lots of rain for hours.",
        "try": "Tap your fingers on a table: pitter-patter, pitter-patter. Say \"{name}!\" and draw a cloud with rain lines in the air using your finger."
      },
      "cloudy": {
        "about": "Cloudy weather means many clouds cover the sky, so the sun may hide or look dim. The air can feel cool and calm. Clouds are not cotton — they are made of tiny water drops floating high up.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Clouds can look like animals, ships, or dragons if you use your imagination! Gray clouds often mean rain might come later, but not always.",
        "try": "Lie on your back or look up. Say \"{name}!\" and tell someone what shape you see in the clouds — or draw your favorite cloud shape."
      },
      "windy": {
        "about": "Windy weather means air moves fast around you. Trees may sway, leaves may fly, and kites can pull on their string. Wind can feel exciting on your face — hold your hat so it does not blow away!",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Wind can spin big windmills to help make electricity in some places! Birds often glide on windy days to save energy.",
        "try": "Blow out slowly like birthday candles — feel the air move. Say \"{name}!\" and wave your arms like a flag in the wind."
      },
      "storm": {
        "about": "A storm is strong weather with heavy wind, rain, and sometimes thunder and lightning. Storms can sound loud, but you are safest inside a strong building with a grown-up. Storms usually pass after a while.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Lightning is a huge spark of electricity in the sky! Thunder is the sound that comes after — light travels faster than sound, so we see flash before we hear boom.",
        "try": "Stay indoors with a grown-up. When you hear thunder, count slowly: \"1 elephant, 2 elephant…\" More seconds means the storm is farther away."
      },
      "snow": {
        "about": "Snow is frozen water that falls in soft white flakes when the air is very cold. It can cover the ground like a blanket and crunch under boots. In snowy places, kids build snowmen and slide on sleds.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Every snowflake has its own pattern — no two are exactly alike! Snow reflects light, so sunny snowy days can feel extra bright.",
        "try": "Pretend snowflakes fall on your tongue — catch one gently in your imagination. Say \"{name}!\" and swirl your fingers down like falling flakes."
      },
      "fog": {
        "about": "Fog is a thick mist close to the ground that makes far things hard to see. It can feel quiet and mysterious, like walking inside a soft gray cloud. Fog often happens in the early morning or near water.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Fog is made of tiny water drops, just like clouds — but low down! Drivers slow down and use special lights because they cannot see far.",
        "try": "Wave your hand slowly in front of your face. Say \"{name}!\" and pretend the world disappears behind your hand like mist."
      },
      "hotDay": {
        "about": "A hot day means the air feels very warm on your skin. You might sweat, feel thirsty, and want cool water or shade. Hot days happen often in summer or in sunny places at noon.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Your body cools itself by sweating — the water on your skin helps heat go away! Light clothes and drinking water help on hot days.",
        "try": "Fan your face with your hand and sip water if a grown-up says yes. Say \"{name}!\" and name one cool place you like — shade, fan, or cold drink."
      },
      "coldDay": {
        "about": "A cold day means the air feels chilly on your cheeks and fingers. You might shiver and want a sweater, socks, or a warm blanket. Cold days are common in winter or early morning.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Some animals grow thicker fur or feathers before cold weather! Your body keeps warm by shivering a little — moving and hugging help too.",
        "try": "Hug yourself gently and say \"Brrr, cold day!\" Rub your hands together slowly to feel warmth. Tell a grown-up if you need another layer."
      },
      "rainbow": {
        "about": "A rainbow is a colorful arc in the sky when sunlight shines through raindrops. You may see red, orange, yellow, green, blue, and more — like a bridge made of light. Rainbows often appear when sun and rain happen together.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? A full rainbow is actually a circle — we usually see only the top part! You cannot walk to the end of a rainbow; it moves as you move.",
        "try": "Say the rainbow colors out loud in order. Draw a big arc in the air with your finger and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "thunder": {
        "about": "Thunder is the deep rumbling sound that comes after lightning in the sky. It can sound loud or scary, but if you are inside with a grown-up, you are usually safe. Thunder tells us a storm is nearby or passing by.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? A big boom can mean lightning was close, and a quiet rumble can mean it was farther away! Counting seconds after a flash helps grown-ups guess the distance.",
        "try": "If a storm is far away, cover your ears softly and whisper \"boom\" like gentle thunder. Say \"{name}!\" and stay calm indoors with someone you trust."
      },
      "drizzle": {
        "about": "Drizzle is very light, gentle rain with tiny drops. The ground may get damp without big puddles. Drizzle can last a long time and feels soft on your skin if you wear a hood.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Drizzle drops are smaller than normal raindrops — they fall slowly! Plants still love drizzle because it waters them bit by bit.",
        "try": "Make tiny tapping sounds on your arm with one finger — drip, drip, drip. Say \"{name}!\" and imagine mist on your cheeks."
      },
      "hail": {
        "about": "Hail is ice that falls in small round pieces during strong storms. Hailstones can be tiny like peas or bigger — they bounce and make loud taps on roofs. Stay indoors when hail falls; it can hurt.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Hail forms when raindrops are tossed up and down inside cold clouds until they freeze in layers! That is why hail looks like little ice balls.",
        "try": "Pretend tiny ice balls bounce in your cupped hands — pat-pat-pat. Say \"{name}!\" and remember: when ice falls from the sky, stay inside with a grown-up."
      },
      "breeze": {
        "about": "A gentle breeze is a soft, light wind that tickles your skin without pushing hard. It can feel cool on a warm day and make leaves rustle quietly. Breezes are friendly wind — not scary storms.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Sailboats and kites use breezes to move! A breeze can carry the smell of flowers or fresh grass to your nose.",
        "try": "Close your eyes and breathe in slowly through your nose. Feel air on your cheeks and say \"{name}!\" Move your fingers like leaves waving."
      },
      "humid": {
        "about": "Humid weather means the air holds lots of water, so it can feel sticky on your skin. Your hair might curl, and sweat may not dry quickly. Humid days often come before rain in many places.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Tropical places near the ocean often feel humid much of the year! Even when you do not see rain, humid air can make you feel damp.",
        "try": "Gently wipe your forehead and say \"Humid — so sticky today!\" Drink water and tell a grown-up how the air feels on your skin."
      },
      "clearSky": {
        "about": "A clear sky has few or no clouds, so you can see the sun, moon, or stars clearly. Clear days feel open and bright; clear nights are perfect for spotting the moon and stars with a grown-up.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Pilots and farmers like clear skies because they can see far! Pollution or smoke can hide stars — dark country skies show the most stars.",
        "try": "Look up (safely, never stare at the bright sun). Trace one star or the moon shape with your finger and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "monsoon": {
        "about": "Monsoon is a special rainy season in India and nearby places. Heavy rains water farms, fill rivers, and cool the hot air. Roads can splash, so kids often play indoors with stories and games.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Monsoon rains help rice and many crops grow — farmers depend on them! Frogs and peacocks are often louder during monsoon.",
        "try": "Listen to real rain or pretend rain sounds with your fingers. Say \"{name}!\" and draw one thing that needs rain — a plant, river, or farm."
      },
      "spring": {
        "about": "Spring is a season after winter when weather warms up and new life appears. Flowers bloom, trees grow fresh leaves, and baby animals may be born. Spring days can be sunny, rainy, or breezy — nature wakes up.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? In spring, days slowly get longer — more daylight for play after school! Many festivals celebrate spring and new beginnings.",
        "try": "Pretend to plant a seed in the ground, water it, and grow tall like a flower. Say \"{name}!\" and name one new thing you see outside in spring."
      },
      "summer": {
        "about": "Summer is the warmest season in many places. Kids may swim, eat cold treats, wear light clothes, and have holidays from school. Summer sun is strong, so shade and water matter.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? In summer, the sun stays up longer in the evening in many countries! Mangoes and ice pops are favorite summer treats in lots of homes.",
        "try": "Pretend to hold a cold juicy fruit and say \"Yum, summer!\" Fan yourself and tell one fun summer plan — pool, park, or picnic."
      },
      "autumn": {
        "about": "Autumn (also called fall) is a season when air turns cooler and some leaves change color — gold, orange, and red — then drift to the ground. Days get shorter and harvest time comes on farms.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Some birds fly long distances in autumn to find warmer places! Squirrels collect nuts to eat when food is harder to find.",
        "try": "Let your arms float down slowly like falling leaves. Say \"{name}!\" and crunch imaginary leaves under your feet — crunch, crunch!"
      },
      "winter": {
        "about": "Winter is the coldest season in many places. You may wear sweaters, drink warm soup, and see frost on grass in the morning. Some regions get snow; others get chilly wind and fog.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? In winter, nights are longer — cozy story time! Animals like bears may sleep more in winter to save energy where it is very cold.",
        "try": "Rub your hands together fast and feel warmth. Say \"{name}!\" and name one cozy winter thing — blanket, soup, or warm socks."
      }
    }
  },
  "days": {
    "description": "Tap any word to read it out loud together.",
    "header": "Seven days of the week",
    "names": {
      "friday": "Friday",
      "monday": "Monday",
      "saturday": "Saturday",
      "sunday": "Sunday",
      "thursday": "Thursday",
      "tuesday": "Tuesday",
      "wednesday": "Wednesday"
    },
    "readTogether": "Point to the word and read it together!",
    "title": "Days of the Week",
    "detail": {
      "monday": {
        "about": "Monday usually starts a new school week for many children. After the weekend, Monday feels like a fresh beginning for learning and new goals.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? The name Monday comes from the Moon in old languages. Many people make Monday to-do lists for the week.",
        "try": "Stretch both arms up like a fresh start and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "tuesday": {
        "about": "Tuesday is the second day of the school week in many places. It is a good day to keep learning habits strong and continue class work.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? In many languages, Tuesday is linked to strength or courage. Small steady effort on Tuesday helps the whole week go smoothly.",
        "try": "Clap 2 times and say \"{name}!\" Then name one class you enjoy."
      },
      "wednesday": {
        "about": "Wednesday sits in the middle of the week. By Wednesday, children have already done part of their work and can feel proud of their progress.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Wednesday is often called mid-week. Many people check plans on Wednesday to stay organized for the rest of the week.",
        "try": "Take one small hop and say \"{name}!\" Then show 3 fingers for mid-week energy."
      },
      "thursday": {
        "about": "Thursday comes after Wednesday and before Friday. It is often a focused day for completing homework, projects, and class practice.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? In some schools, Thursday has special activity periods like sports, art, or music.",
        "try": "Give yourself a thumbs-up and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "friday": {
        "about": "Friday is often the last school day before the weekend. Many children feel excited because rest and family time are near.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Friday evenings are popular for family meals and fun plans in many homes.",
        "try": "Do a tiny happy dance and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "saturday": {
        "about": "Saturday is a weekend day in many places. It can be used for play, hobbies, family visits, markets, or extra rest.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Parks, playgrounds, and sports classes are often busiest on Saturdays.",
        "try": "Say \"{name}!\" and name one fun activity you want to do this weekend."
      },
      "sunday": {
        "about": "Sunday is often a quiet family day before a new week starts. Many people rest, pray, travel, or prepare school things for Monday.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? In many calendars, Sunday is marked as a special rest day.",
        "try": "Take a deep breath, smile softly, and say \"{name}!\""
      }
    }
  },
  "months": {
    "description": "Tap any word to read it out loud together.",
    "header": "Twelve months of the year",
    "names": {
      "april": "April",
      "august": "August",
      "december": "December",
      "february": "February",
      "january": "January",
      "july": "July",
      "june": "June",
      "march": "March",
      "may": "May",
      "november": "November",
      "october": "October",
      "september": "September"
    },
    "readTogether": "Point to the word and read it together!",
    "title": "Months of the Year",
    "detail": {
      "aboutTemplate": "{name} is one of the twelve months in a year. Learning month names helps children understand birthdays, seasons, school events, and festivals.",
      "funFactTemplate": "Did you know? Months repeat in the same order every year. Knowing {name} helps you talk about when events happened or will happen.",
      "tryTemplate": "Say \"{name}\" slowly. Clap once for each syllable and tell a grown-up one event in {name}.",
      "january": {
        "about": "January is the first month of the year. It often feels like a new beginning with new calendars, school terms, and goals.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? New Year celebrations happen at the start of January in many countries.",
        "try": "Say \"{name}!\" and pretend to flip to a new calendar page."
      },
      "february": {
        "about": "February is the second month and the shortest month of the year. It usually has 28 days, and sometimes 29 in a leap year.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Leap year comes every four years and gives February one extra day.",
        "try": "Count to 2 on your fingers and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "march": {
        "about": "March is the third month. In many places, weather starts changing and spring signs appear, while in India many festivals may happen around this time.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? In many regions, school exams or final terms begin around March.",
        "try": "Say \"{name}!\" and pretend flowers are starting to bloom."
      },
      "april": {
        "about": "April is the fourth month. Many places feel warmer in April, and trees and gardens become greener.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? April is often linked with changing weather, from sunny mornings to sudden rain.",
        "try": "Draw a tiny flower in the air and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "may": {
        "about": "May is the fifth month and is often hot in many parts of India. Many children enjoy summer holidays during this month.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Mango season is strong in May in many places, making it a favorite month for fruit lovers.",
        "try": "Say \"{name}!\" and pretend to fan yourself in warm weather."
      },
      "june": {
        "about": "June is the sixth month. In many schools, new sessions or classes begin around June, and rains may start in some regions.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? June has long daylight in many countries, giving more bright time in the evening.",
        "try": "Say \"{name}!\" and mimic carrying a school bag proudly."
      },
      "july": {
        "about": "July is the seventh month. In many places, monsoon rain becomes stronger and children carry umbrellas and raincoats.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Farmers welcome July rains because crops need steady water.",
        "try": "Tap your fingers like rain and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "august": {
        "about": "August is the eighth month. It is a festive month in India with patriotic celebrations and school cultural activities.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? India celebrates Independence Day in August.",
        "try": "Wave an imaginary flag and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "september": {
        "about": "September is the ninth month. Schools are usually active with studies, assignments, and regular routines.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? In many places, weather starts shifting slowly after peak monsoon.",
        "try": "Say \"{name}!\" and open an imaginary notebook to study."
      },
      "october": {
        "about": "October is the tenth month and often brings pleasant weather in many regions. It is also a festive period in India for many families.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Several major Indian festivals often fall in October, making it colorful and joyful.",
        "try": "Smile wide, say \"{name}!\" and pretend to light a festival lamp safely with a grown-up."
      },
      "november": {
        "about": "November is the eleventh month. The air can feel cooler in many places, and schools continue with projects and events.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? November often has clear skies in many regions after rainy months.",
        "try": "Wrap your arms like a light sweater hug and say \"{name}!\""
      },
      "december": {
        "about": "December is the twelfth and last month of the year. Many families celebrate holidays, review memories, and prepare for a new year.",
        "funFact": "Did you know? Year-end celebrations happen in December across many cultures and communities.",
        "try": "Count down 3, 2, 1 and say \"{name}!\" with a cheerful clap."
      }
    }
  },
  "family": {
